{"Key":"c4a70ed0d505d6308a1548703f0f0c30","Value":{"Backend":"builtin","Platform":"rss","Success":true,"Data":"Title: The Go Blog\nDescription: \n\n1. Arch-specific SIMD in Go\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/archsimd\n   Published: 2026-10-02T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.27 introduces experimental arch-specific SIMD vector APIs\n\n2. Platform-independent SIMD in Go\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/simd-experiment\n   Published: 2026-09-24T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.27 adds an experimental platform-agnostic SIMD API\n\n3. Size-Specialized Memory Allocation\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/size-specialized-allocations\n   Published: 2026-09-16T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.27 improves performance of small allocations using size-specialized allocation functions.\n\n4. Goroutine Leak Profiles\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/goroutine-leak-profiles\n   Published: 2026-09-02T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.27 includes new goroutine leak profiles.\n\n5. Generic Methods\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/generic-methods\n   Published: 2026-08-26T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.27 adds generic methods—a highly desired language feature.\n\n6. Go 1.27 is released\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/go1.27\n   Published: 2026-08-19T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.27 adds generic methods, encoding/json/v2 package, uuid package, faster memory allocation, goroutine leak profiles, and more.\n\n7. Introducing the pkg.go.dev API\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/pkgsite-api\n   Published: 2026-05-21T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Introducing the new programmatic API for pkg.go.dev, allowing developers to fetch package and module data directly.\n\n8. Type Construction and Cycle Detection\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/type-construction-and-cycle-detection\n   Published: 2026-03-24T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: Go 1.26 simplifies type construction and enhances cycle detection for certain kinds of recursive types.\n\n9. //go:fix inline and the source-level inliner\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/inliner\n   Published: 2026-03-10T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: How Go 1.26\u0026#39;s source-level inliner works, and how it can help you with self-service API migrations.\n\n10. Allocating on the Stack\n   Link: https://go.dev/blog/allocation-optimizations\n   Published: 2026-02-27T00:00:00+00:00\n   Summary: A description of some of the recent changes to do allocations on the stack instead of the heap.\n\n","Error":"","Latency":0,"Timestamp":"2026-10-08T11:02:28.1383873+07:00"},"CreatedAt":"2026-10-08T11:02:28.1383873+07:00","ExpiresAt":"2026-10-08T11:07:28.1383873+07:00","Hits":0}